CHOICES FOR MISSING-VALUE BRANCHING AND EXECUTION.

An English Major C decision record.
Status: competing designs RETAINED; no optimized sequence selected.
This document records the earlier R-specific comparison question.
It does NOT prescribe the semantics of the first English Major C
unknown-entry example, nor impose an R type system, R NA/NaN,
or an ICK compiler stage upon it. The later human correction
is recorded in "Why the first compiler emits assembly directly.english".
Example: R's sum(..., na.rm=TRUE), notably the integer path called
"isum" in the previously discussed R implementation.
Architecture: symbolic ARMv7-A T32, unless another architecture is
explicitly named. Full ARM source and the initial permitted operation
grant are under ../architecture/armv7-a/t32/.

PURPOSE OF THIS RECORD.

The purpose of this document is not to show a beginner how to write a
loop. The purpose is to preserve, with lawyer-like precision, every
identified design alternative, all relevant constraints and effects,
the evidence that would distinguish them, and whether a selection has
actually been justified. Rejected options must remain visible.

This is the reconstructed set of alternatives identified in the
October 10-11, 2026 discussions. It is not presented as an exhaustive
transcript of every unrecorded idea anyone might have considered.
The actual disassembled compiled R routine and its machine-level
execution evidence must be examined before declaring one method
superior. Invented assembly is not evidence.

PART ONE. THE REQUIRED BEHAVIOR AND ITS OPEN BOUNDARIES.

The example has a sequence of elements and an option named "na.rm".
A correct specialized "remove missing values" path skips precisely
those elements that R specifies as missing for the relevant vector
type, while preserving R's observable sum behavior. "Missing" and
"equal to zero" are not synonyms.

For R integer vectors, the representation of NA_INTEGER and the rules
for arithmetic overflow matter. For real vectors, distinguish R NA,
ordinary NaN, infinities, signed zero and any floating-point
environment effects that prove observable. Integer and floating
cases must NOT be conflated. Empty vectors, long vectors, ALTREP,
dispatch to a different implementation, and argument evaluation
must not silently disappear from the correctness contract.

Before writing an optimization, bind the exact R version, routine,
vector type, accumulator width, missing-value predicate,
overflow policy, return type and observable conditions. A
syntactically similar small C function is not the R implementation.

PART TWO. THE DESIGN ALTERNATIVES.

Candidate A: Check missingness and branch around the accumulation.

Description: after loading an element, determine whether it is
missing; take a conditional branch when the element should not
contribute. Otherwise add it. The ordinary loop branch remains.

Possible advantage: no accumulator arithmetic for a skipped
element; straightforward match to high-level conditions; no
extra data conversion before addition.

Possible disadvantage: an additional data-dependent branch per
tested element. Its effect depends on probability and correlation
of missingness, code arrangement, and the exact predictor.

Conditions: preserve the flags produced by the comparison until
the branch consumes them. A branch on equal after CMP depends
on Z; writing flags in between can change behavior.

Status: an explicit baseline candidate, not proven fastest.

Candidate B: Test "na.rm" repeatedly inside the loop.

Description: on encountering missing input, check whether the
caller requested removal or propagation of the missing result.

Possible advantage: one shared routine can handle both policies;
less duplicated source or generated code.

Possible disadvantage: repeatedly performs work based on an option
that often remains fixed throughout the entire call. This can
add comparisons, loads or branches even if they are predictable.

Conditions: the option must in fact be invariant for the duration
of the loop. Any side effects or externally mutable policy must
be accounted for before moving its test.

Status: available baseline candidate; actual R call graph
and assembly must establish whether, where, and how it appears.

Candidate C: Select a specialized loop once before processing the data.

Description: inspect "na.rm" once and enter one of two distinct
loops, one where missing values are ignored and one where they
cause the required non-removal behavior. Do not re-test the
unchanging option on every iteration.

Possible advantage: moves a condition out of the hot loop; can
permit simpler inner-loop code and improve scheduling.

Possible disadvantage: two loop bodies take more instruction-cache
space and add a one-time dispatch. It may reduce opportunities
for other optimizations or benefit only sufficiently large inputs.

Conditions: exact equivalence for long-vector and overflow paths;
correct treatment of values and ALTREP iteration. Source-level
specialization is not instruction prefetch and does not direct
the branch predictor to "preload" another target.

Status: candidate, not accepted without verified call traces
and target benchmarks.

Candidate D: Arrange the common path as sequential fall-through.

Description: choose branch direction so common data follows
the adjacent instruction stream and uncommon cases transfer
control. This may mean a branch over the add, or its inverse,
depending on distributions and encoded instruction constraints.

Possible advantage: can reduce taken branches for a known
distribution; may affect locality and processor fetching.

Possible disadvantage: distributions differ. Static branch
layout does not ensure a particular processor predicts it
correctly. The resulting code may grow or need more instructions.

Conditions: hold the same input/output, flags and memory effects;
include branch reach, two/four-byte encodings, alignment and
instruction-cache behavior in actual comparisons.

Status: candidate; distribution-dependent, unmeasured here.

Candidate E: Branchless conditional contribution of zero.

Description: derive a value equal to the original input when
it counts and zero when it should be omitted; add that value
unconditionally. The selection might be a mask constructed from
the predicate, a conditional select where supported, or another
verified sequence.

Possible advantage: removes an unpredictable data-dependent
branch from an inner loop.

Possible disadvantage: comparison, mask construction,
selection and addition still execute. On predictable inputs
they can be slower than a branch. A wrong mask can mishandle
missing-value encodings; conditionalizing a result does not
necessarily suppress faulting memory accesses or other effects.

Conditions: mask widths and signedness, arithmetic overflow,
flag preservation, constant-time properties if relevant, and
architecture-specific instruction selection must be demonstrated.

Status: candidate; not presumed faster and not an existing
single native T32 CMOV instruction.

Candidate F: T32 If-Then conditional execution.

Description: on a legal T32 target, use an IT instruction to
condition the permitted following operation or operations,
rather than selecting an explicit separate branch in some cases.

Possible advantage: can avoid an explicit branch around one or
a few operations. The construction is especially interesting
for the "add if not missing" case.

Possible disadvantage: IT has at most four following slots,
strict condition and branch placement rules, and restrictions
on flag-setting and other instructions. Added IT instructions
are themselves work; behavior and timing remain target-specific.
For another ISA revision, these forms may be more restricted.

Conditions: use a legal IT encoding and a permitted conditional
instruction, with matching condition flags and no illegal block
composition. Do not mistake T32 IT semantics for unrestricted
A32 predication.

Status: candidate with architectural legality to be verified
for the exact emitted instruction pair.

Candidate G: A32 predicated arithmetic.

Description: in ARM A32 state, a conditional arithmetic
instruction may express "add when condition holds" without
a preceding explicit control-transfer instruction.

Possible advantage: instruction predication may fit a compact
scalar case.

Possible disadvantage: this changes the instruction-state
contract and cannot be silently emitted by the current
T32-only English Major C profile. It is not necessarily faster.

Conditions: explicit A32 machine grant, instruction mapping,
interworking permissions and independent result verification.

Status: excluded from THIS permitted operation profile, but
retained as a cross-architecture option.

Candidate H: T32 compare-and-branch-on-zero / nonzero.

Description: use CBZ or CBNZ to directly test a general register
and conditionally jump, without a separate flag-setting CMP.

Possible advantage: short instruction and no dependence on
APSR comparison flags for the test itself.

Possible disadvantage: R's missing integer sentinel is not
generically zero. Testing for missingness would first require
a correctly formed zero/nonzero predicate, and that conversion
can consume instructions. The encoded branches are forward and
range-constrained, and are not permitted inside an IT block.

Conditions: enforce the exact legal register and target ranges.
Never directly replace an NA_INTEGER equality test with
a zero test unless the conversion is explicitly implemented.

Status: candidate for suitable predicates, not a general
replacement for comparing against R's missing marker.

Candidate I: T32 table branches using byte or halfword entries.

Description: use TBB or TBH to read a small data table and
choose a halfword-scaled branch target, possibly for several
cases instead of repeated comparisons.

Possible advantage: useful for finite-state dispatch with
several possible destinations; tables make the difference
between code and control-flow data explicit.

Possible disadvantage: additional data access, table storage,
bounds discipline and indirect control flow are usually
unattractive for a simple yes/no NA decision. A table entry
is DATA used to calculate an execution address, not itself an
instruction being executed.

Conditions: table base, index, entry width, branch base, legal
destinations and the exact target processor must be specified.
The mere presence of TBB/TBH in the ISA is not justification.

Status: considered and not selected for a two-way branch
without comparative evidence.

Candidate J: Branch threading, early exit, and skip patterns.

Description: alter the layout to transfer directly to a
known eventual destination, exit the loop once the semantics
permit, or avoid an intermediate jump. Historical processors
also offered skip-the-next-instruction idioms such as the
PDP-8 skip mechanisms.

Possible advantage: can avoid redundant work or shorten
certain control paths; early exits can save processing when
the result has become predetermined.

Possible disadvantage: semantic early exit for integer NA
propagation is different from the case where na.rm=TRUE and
later values still matter. Historical skip-next mechanisms
are NOT generic T32 facilities and cannot be emitted by
naming them. Branch threading can increase code size.

Conditions: exact R result for every remaining input class;
no skipped observable side effects, errors or iterator effects.
No unsupported native "skip next" instruction may be invented.

Status: separately retained family of candidates.

Candidate K: SIMD/vector processing.

Description: on explicitly verified optional vector hardware,
process several elements together, form valid masks for missing
values, and combine results according to the required R
semantics and overflow handling.

Possible advantage: more elements processed per instruction.

Possible disadvantage: mask and reduction overhead, remainder
handling, alignment, different association order for floating
point, and presence/absence of NEON or another extension.
A vector instruction set is NOT granted by the current scalar
English Major C profile.

Conditions: exact target extension feature, floating-point
semantics, integer overflow rule and independent vector/scalar
comparison over hostile inputs.

Status: out of current scope but recorded as an option.

Candidate L: x86-64 conditional move and other foreign-ISA forms.

Description: a CMOV-like instruction may replace a conditional
control transfer with a conditional register update on an
appropriate x86-64 target.

Possible advantage: different prediction-versus-extra-work
tradeoff on that architecture.

Possible disadvantage: it is not an ARMv7-A T32 instruction;
cannot be treated as the ARM assembly produced by this project's
independent English-to-assembly compiler.
Even on x86 it can be inferior to a predictable branch.

Conditions: a distinct, versioned x86-64 machine profile.

Status: foreign-target candidate, not in this ARM grant.

Candidate M: Processor branch prediction, fetch and speculation.

Description: the physical core may predict branch direction
and target, fetch upcoming instruction bytes before they are
architecturally required, and later discard work if prediction
was wrong.

Possible advantage: predicted branches may have lower visible
latency; layout and repeated patterns can matter.

Possible disadvantage: a misprediction costs work, and behavior
is implementation-specific. Architectural fetch and PC operand
semantics cannot be substituted with guessed pipeline depths.

Conditions: actual processor identification and reproducible
measurements. The language-level execution index remains the
architectural next address, not an enumeration of prefetched
instructions. This is not an independently programmable
"preload the branch" operation in the first profile.

Status: hardware consideration, not a chosen source strategy.

PART THREE. IMPORTANT NON-EQUIVALENCES.

1. One-time dispatch on "na.rm" does not equal branch prediction.
2. Bitwise selection does not equal permission to fetch from a
   protected address and suppress the fault after the fact.
3. ARM's T32 IT mechanism is not full A32 predication.
4. A TBB/TBH entry is data controlling a later instruction address;
   it is not automatically executable data.
5. A signed R NA sentinel is not the integer zero; predicates
   must use the correct representation and type.
6. Fewer branches is not automatically fewer cycles, less energy,
   lower latency, or better cache behavior.
7. A change to missing-value handling cannot be accepted merely
   because the result matches a simple non-NA example.
8. A generated assembly listing is not itself a measured execution
   trace; a compiled artifact is not proof of actual CPU execution.

PART FOUR. EVIDENCE THAT MUST PRECEDE A PERFORMANCE DECISION.

The previously specified x86-64 artifact investigation targeted:
- Debian R 4.5.0-3, r-base-core_4.5.0-3_amd64.deb
  SHA256 6c529a906d2a26cf78e6c24eaa96e0bbd061b032e6c824f9a61e5f313353b155
- matching r-base-core-dbgsym_4.5.0-3_amd64.deb
  SHA256 46ed2eb150470d1b8ae413aa0b6171cf827787644d

Those identifiers belong to an earlier examination request. This new
architecture document has NOT fetched, disassembled, checked symbols
against, compiled, or executed either artifact; it may not borrow an
unsupported "verified optimal" result from that request.

For any proposed equivalence or speed advantage, the test SHALL bind:
  A. actual implementation source, compiled routine and symbol/IR identity;
  B. exact instruction bytes, boundaries, ISA state and disassembly;
  C. comparison oracle for R integer/real NA, NaN, infinities, overflow,
     empty/long inputs and other relevant observable behavior;
  D. exact English Major C compiler revision and emitted assembly,
     plus independently declared ARM assembler/linker provenance;
     ICK is not an English Major C compiler stage;
  E. complete actual-target CPU/SoC and instruction-feature manifest;
  F. test sets with zero missingness, all missingness, rare missingness,
     approximately balanced missingness, random versus clustered missing
     values, and short versus long inputs;
  G. instruction count, conditional/taken-branch counts, misprediction
     evidence where counters are supported, execution time and variations;
  H. separate flags/IT legality, memory fault and branch-range checks;
  I. tests where a known-wrong missing-value predicate, flag clobber,
     incorrect dispatch, illegal IT block and faulty table target
     MUST be rejected by the verifier.

A measured claim can only be made at the verified target and input
distribution. Results on one microarchitecture do not prove an
universal ARM or x86 rule.

PART FIVE. CURRENT DECISION.

The choice remains OPEN.

The initial architecture profile permits CMP, conditional B, CBZ,
CBNZ and IT, among other operations, to allow candidate
implementations to be expressed. The presence of those
operations does NOT select among them.

The formal machine model and long operation names are settled
only as draft source vocabulary. The optimized R loop, its
compiler lowering, the original compiled R routine and the
required benchmark acceptance remain independent tasks.

